{\sffamily\fontsize{7.8}{8.8}\selectfont
\textbf{Problem.} Find the minimum legal moves from any legal three-peg
configuration to any other legal configuration. The target is fixed globally;
the recursive state is the three named pegs.\par\vspace{1.8mm}
\textbf{Example 1 (used below).}
$L=(2), M=(3), R=(1)$ to $L=(3), M=(2), R=(1)$ $\rightarrow 7$ moves.\par\vspace{1mm}
\textbf{Example 2.}
$L=(3,2,1), M=(), R=()$ to $L=(), M=(), R=(3,2,1)$
$\rightarrow 7$ moves.\par\vspace{1.8mm}
There are $3^3=27$ legal states: assigning each disk to a peg uniquely determines
its position because disks on a peg must be ordered.}

