The membership line above a node records the actual sets. The circle records only
the parameters received by that recursive call.
\par\vspace{2mm}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.42}
\begin{tabular}{>{\bfseries}p{19mm}p{51mm}p{43mm}p{43mm}}
\textcolor{ink}{Form} & \textcolor{ink}{Meaning of positions} &
\textcolor{ink}{History $\mathcal H$} & \textcolor{ink}{History $\mathcal K$}\\
\hline
$F(i,a,b)$ & permanent Set 1 / Set 2 &
$F(4,4,2)$ & $F(5,6,4)$\\
$G(i,L,S)$ & sort after every choice so $L\ge S$ &
$G(4,4,2)$; roles switched twice & $G(5,6,4)$\\
$H(i,C,O)$ & choosing other makes it current &
$H(4,4,2)$; roles switched twice & $H(5,4,6)$; now $C<O$\\
\end{tabular}
\par\vspace{3mm}
\textbf{History $\mathcal H$:}
$1\to A,\ 2\to B,\ 3\to A$, giving
$A=\{1,3\}$ and $B=\{2\}$.
It exposes the normalization swap in $G$ and the action-driven swap in $H$.
\par\vspace{2mm}
\textbf{History $\mathcal K$:}
$1,2,3\to A,\ 4\to B$, giving sums $(6,4)$.
$F$ preserves $(A,B)$, $G$ stores $(6,4)$ by magnitude, and $H$ stores
$(4,6)$ because the recipient of the ``other'' action becomes current.
\par\vspace{3mm}
